# `morale` (spirit or confidence) is often written in place of `moral` (lesson or ethics).
expr modifier [compass, dilemma, dilemmas, duty, duties, fiber, fibre, hazard, hazards, obligation, obligations, philosophy, principle, principles, relativism, responsibility, responsibilities, standard, standards, turpitude, value, values, victory, victories]
expr main [<(morale of [the, this, that] story), morale>, <(morale high ground), morale>, <(morale @modifier), morale>]

let message "Did you mean `moral`? `Morale` refers to spirit or confidence; `moral` relates to ethics or a lesson."
let description "Corrects `morale` to `moral` in expressions about ethics or the lesson of a story."
let kind "Malapropism"
let becomes "moral"
let strategy "MatchCase"

test "Morale of the Story. Don't Trust Banks. Trust yourself." "Moral of the Story. Don't Trust Banks. Trust yourself."
test "What is the morale of this story?" "What is the moral of this story?"
test "We claimed the morale high ground." "We claimed the moral high ground."
test "They faced a difficult morale dilemma." "They faced a difficult moral dilemma."
test "Strong morale values guide the organization." "Strong moral values guide the organization."
test "It was only a morale victory for the visitors." "It was only a moral victory for the visitors."

allows "The morale of the team improved after the win."
allows "Low morale affected the whole department."
allows "Morale among the troops remained high."
